this week president biden laid out a  very ambitious goal he wants 70 of the  world's population to be vaccinated  against covet in the next year but for  many nations that is a huge challenge in  the tiny african kingdom of lesotho the  official death toll there is around 400  but it's thought to be much much higher  higher deborah powder traveled there to  see the difficulties firsthand  especially when it comes to rural  communities which just are very hard to  reach  as dawn breaks pilot matthew monson gets  ready for a busy day ahead he makes the  final checks  before flying health workers on the  pandemic front line to the most remote  parts of lesotho it's not called the  mountain kingdom for nothing but that  makes places like this really hard to  reach and often the weather is volatile  as well hampering efforts to get those  vaccines into arms  dr justin tsushir is supervising the  covert vaccine rollout from the skies  he's skeptical about achieving a 70  global vaccination rate within one year  the suggestion of president biden to  vaccinate 70 percent of population in  african continent  this can be really a big challenge  lesotho believes it will get enough  vaccines to inoculate its nearly 1.6  million adult population but acquiring  them is only half the battle sometimes  the challenge is how we can reach them  in the village of leba king only five  people have turned up for a shot one of  them is 69 year old kapigana mapondo so  it took you two hours to walk here two  hours  people have to cross mountains and  rivers to get to clinics donkeys are the  most common form of transport if you're  lucky enough to own one  our next stop is manamaning sometimes  the wind is so bad flying is impossible  but today there's a different  obstruction preventing us from touching  down  sure well that was a little bit of an  interesting landing we had to abort the  first one because there were too many  sheep on the runway just one of the  hazards of flying in the suture  but a hazardous landing is only the  start of the obstacles facing these  health workers we have to move out in  the village to find people from their  homes so instead of waiting for people  to come to the clinic they're taking the  vaccines to them but like so many  far-flung african regions the pace of  vaccinations is excruciatingly slow and  while wealthier nations roll out third  covert booster shots they remain  dangerously exposed to a looming fourth  wave  for cbs mornings debra patter lusutu
 

